maya-support: customer Lumina hit repeated tool-call failures after migrating to our OA-compatible ingestion. They see our endpoint returning 400 with validation error. stack: SchemaValidationError: arguments.orderId: expected string but got number. customer says this used to work before.

ben-eng: can you paste the function schema and the exact model response (raw)?

maya-support: here's the function schema they registered (trimmed):
```json
{
  "name": "getOrder",
  "parameters": {
    "type": "object",
    "properties": {
      "orderId": {
        "type": "string"
      },
      "includeItems": {
        "type": "boolean"
        }
    },
    "required": ["orderId"]
  }
}
```
and this is the model's tool_call chunk we got from the customer (raw):
```json
{
  "tool_call": {
    "name": "getOrder",
    "arguments": {
      "payload": {
        "orderId": 123456,
        "includeItems": "true"
      },
      "meta": {
        "receivedAt": "2026-03-01T12:23:45Z"
      }
    }
  }
}
```
maya-support: note two mismatches: 1) `arguments` is an object with a nested `payload` (we expect direct keys), 2) orderId is a number not string, includeItems is string "true", and enums/casing might differ elsewhere.

li-ml: this looks like an envelope-style tool_call some systems output (payload/meta). OA spec usually has `arguments` as a JSON-string or object of top-level args. We need a compat path: accept both top-level and envelope.payload. Also coerce basic scalar types when obviously convertible.

olivia-se: customer insists they didn't change their client. They suspect the model or a middleware added envelope. Could be downstream LLM plugin that wraps responses. We should confirm request_id 1d4f8c from their logs.

runtime-oncall: short-term mitigation: add a normalization layer in the request pipeline to flatten `arguments.payload` if present and coerce booleans/nums to expected types when schema indicates. Example pseudo:
```js
// normalize tool_call arguments
const args = resp.tool_call.arguments.payload || resp.tool_call.arguments;
if (typeof schema.properties.orderId.type === 'string' && typeof args.orderId === 'number') {
  args.orderId = String(args.orderId);
}
args.includeItems = (args.includeItems === 'true' || args.includeItems === true);
```
This should unblock customers quickly. We'll gate the normalizer under a feature flag.

ben-eng: agree. please also accept when `arguments` is a stringified JSON (some OA responses do that). Add a try-parse step.

li-ml: I'll own the compat heuristic. Plan: 1) flatten `arguments.payload` if exists, 2) if `arguments` is string -> try JSON.parse, 3) coerce scalars using the function schema map, 4) record a compatibility metric and the detected variant so we can measure incidence. Will submit PR and canary it to a few hosts.

maya-support: repro steps + customer curl (redacted token):
```bash
curl -X POST https://api.redwood.ai/v1/chat/completions \
  -H 'Authorization: Bearer sk-xxxx' \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"model":"oai-compat-v1","messages":[{"role":"user","content":"Get order 123456"}],"functions":[/* schema above */],"function_call":"auto"}'
```
logs: request_id=1d4f8c, received_at=2026-03-01T12:23:45Z, route=hosted-eu-2

support-bot: Ticket SUP-4521 created: "OA tool_call envelope causing validation failures for Lumina" assigned to eng-ml.

maya-support: customer asked for ETA and workaround; they can enable older endpoint if needed.

li-ml: ETA: I can push a compat patch to canary by EOD tomorrow and roll to global within 48-72h after verification. Workaround for customer: use `?compat=legacy-toolcall` param or we can flip the feature-flag on their org. I'll coordinate with runtime-oncall for canary.

ben-eng: please add a regression test for cases: stringified-arguments, arguments.payload nesting, number->string coercion, boolean-string coercion, and invalid enum casing.

runtime-oncall: will pre-create the feature-flag and add a short-term deploy hook. I'll post the canary link once ready.

maya-support: thanks — I'll tell Lumina the plan and offer org flag flip until the global rollout.

li-ml: noted. will include telemetry so we can later tighten rules or remove org-level flag once safe.

support-bot: SUP-4521 status: acknowledged -> in progress.

maya-support: tagging @ben-eng @li-ml for visibility. :thumbsup:
